{
  "id": "Compound::DB00281 - Gene::6328",
  "verbalization": "Lidocaine, Prilocaine, Ropivacaine, and Mepivacaine are all amide-based anesthetics that share a common structural feature: they belong to the class of monocarboxylic acid amides. This shared classification suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the binding of these compounds to the Scn3a channel, a key ion channel involved in neuronal excitation. The evidence from the visible paths indicates that these amides bind to Scn3a, with the lowest common ancestor of all these entities being the amide class. This suggests a general mechanism of action where amide compounds interact with Scn3a, potentially modulating its function. The consistent presence of amide-based anesthetics in the paths and their binding to Scn3a implies a broad pharmacological relationship, possibly involving the modulation of neuronal activity through Scn3a. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Lidocaine and its analogs act through a common pathway involving Scn3a, highlighting the role of amide compounds in influencing ion channel function.",
